## [x] 7. Teach huqan.policy the MCP tool classification
Done. lib/mcp-tool-policy.js answers for any tool the gate adapter classifies,
and returns null otherwise so toolPolicy.js's fail-closed handling is intact.
agent.inspectToolPolicy consults it first.

Live through MCP now:
  huqan.learn  -> internal/review        (matches the gate that runs it)
  huqan.plan   -> internal/allow
  huqan.agent  -> internal/dry_run_only
  learn        -> internal/allow         (the agent-loop question, unchanged)
  huqan.notatool, shell.exec -> external/block, still fail-closed

huqan.learn and bare learn now differ on purpose: an MCP call to learn is
gated review by AB4, while learn as an agent-loop step is allowed. Two
questions, each answered by its own authority -- not one question with two
answers, which is what item 2 removed.

## [x] 8. Add an HTTP operator approval path
Done, and the premise was partly wrong -- corrected in item 11 below.

GET  /api/v2/memory-approvals
POST /api/v2/memory-approvals/{id}/decision

Both require the API key *and* a separate operator token in
x-huqan-operator-token, checked constant-time against HUQAN_MCP_OPERATOR_TOKEN.
Serving them behind the API key alone would have let whoever may propose a
memory write also approve it, which is the separation mcpServer.js already
enforces by withholding huqan.approve from tools/list. Unconfigured, the route
answers 404, matching /api/a2a/exchange and the external-client route.

It delegates to the same huqan.approvals / huqan.approve tools the CLI drives,
so the receipts and audit events are the tested ones by construction.

lib/http/optional-boundaries.js composes this with the A2A boundary, because
server.js may not grow a line per route family any more than per route: it is
at exactly the same 1118 lines it was before this route existed.

## [x] 9. A PREVENTS edge contradicts the sentence that states it
Found while verifying item 3; not caused by it. Fixed in lib/verify.js: the
stored-PREVENTS contradiction branch now fires only when the incoming claim is
affirmative, so a claim that asserts the same prevention verifies. The branch's
original purpose is kept and tested.

Still open from the same output: the contradiction evidence string is Turkish
prose ("... ile celiskiyor: ..."). Folded into item 10.

Isolated to the verifier -- edges written straight to the graph, no plugin, no
ingest, no admission in the path:

  addEdge('exercise','heart disease','PREVENTS',{strength:0.8,confidence:0.95})
  verify('Exercise prevents heart disease')
    -> status "contradicted", confidence 0.95
       evidence: exercise --[PREVENTS]--> heart disease ile celiskiyor:
                 "Exercise prevents heart disease"

  addEdge('smoking','lung cancer','CAUSES',{strength:0.8,confidence:0.95})
  verify('Smoking causes lung cancer')
    -> status "verified", confidence 0.95

So three of the four advertised causal relations verify correctly and PREVENTS
inverts: asserting it produces a confident contradiction of itself. This is the
core value proposition failing on a relation the README lists, with
"Vaccination prevents disease" as one of its own worked examples.

Almost certainly polarity handling: PREVENTS is a negative-polarity relation
and the contradiction check appears to treat the stated claim as its own
negation. Fix the polarity, then keep a test per causal relation so the four
cannot diverge again.

Second, smaller finding on the same output: the contradiction evidence string
is Turkish ("... ile celiskiyor: ..."). That is prose, not the status
vocabulary of item 4, so it needs its own pass.

## [x] 10. Turkish prose on English API surfaces
Distinct from item 4, which is about the status *vocabulary*. This is free
prose emitted alongside a correct English status, e.g. the contradiction
evidence built in lib/verify.js:

  "exercise --[PREVENTS]--> heart disease ile celiskiyor: \"...\""

Also the MCP tool descriptions, whose input examples still read
"kedi hayvandir" -- visible to every Claude and Cursor user in tools/list.

Done for the emitted surfaces: lib/verify.js evidence sentences and the MCP
tool descriptions. A scan of 312 Turkish string literals across 48 runtime
files found the rest are inputs the reader accepts on purpose -- command words
in lib/command-parser.js, linguistic data in lib/contradiction-rules.js and
lib/type-lattice.js, the legacy statuses in lib/verify-status-vocabulary.js --
and the standalone Turkish demo in scripts/egitim-demo.js. Those stay, per
RFC-001 decision 7.

Three of the verify.js strings also carried U+FFFD: their Turkish characters
had already been destroyed, so callers read "Say?sal kar??la?t?rma". They were
in numeric-comparison branches no test drove, which is why they survived every
green run. test/english-api-surface-prose.test.js now guards both the rendered
evidence and the source.

## [ ] 11. A /upload admission cannot be resolved by any surface
Found while doing item 8, and it corrects that item's premise. The gap is not
"HTTP cannot approve what /upload proposed" -- it is that *nothing* can.

  POST /upload {"text":"..."}
    -> outcome "review", approvalStatus "pending", graphWrite false,
       provenanceId prov_..., receiptId madm_receipt_...,
       approvalId ""            <- empty

The empty approvalId is the finding. Checked against a live server:

  the approval store contains only MCP-sourced records
    tools:   {"huqan.learn": 50}
    sources: mcp
  GET /api/candidate-claims?status=pending  ->  {"items":[],"total":0}

So /upload is propose-and-refuse, not propose-and-queue: it records that a
write was attempted and denied, with provenance and a review receipt, and
leaves nothing that can later be approved. The CLI's "onaylar"/"onayla" list
MCP tool approvals, so they cannot resolve it either.

That may well be the intended V4-B2 boundary -- HTTP action authority is
scoped to the ingest kinds, and those *do* queue approvals. If it is intended,
the /upload response should say so rather than returning approvalStatus
"pending" with no id, which reads as "queued, resolve it later". If it is not,
/upload needs to queue an approval the way huqan.learn does.

Decide which, then make the response honest. Do not change the gate itself
without deciding first.
